  "query_preview": ".. A closed upper low continues to progress east across \nAZ and NM today with the left-exit region of an upper jet streak \nnudging its way into southern UT this afternoon. While ascent \nremains modest across southern UT through much of the event, cool \nmid-level temperatures (-10C to -12C) coupled with left-exit region \njet periphery favoring diffluence across southern UT will favor \nconvective development this afternoon. At the surface, a low will \ncontinue progressing east across southern AZ into southern NM, \nphasing in with the remnants of Hurricane Polo through the day. This \nwill yield modest wrap around moisture return, serving to raise \nPWATs to the 0.8-1.0\" range, with some areas seeing localized values \nto 1.2\" of water. This raises some concern for flash flooding across \nsouthern UT this afternoon into the evening hours, though \nuncertainty does exist given lack of widespread deep moisture \nprofiles and weaker ascent potentially resulting in widespread light \nstratiform rain versus deeper convection capable of heavy rainfall. \nRegardless, the SPC has much of southern UT outlined within a\nmoderate risk (level 3/4) for excessive rainfall this afternoon. \n\nFollowing today, zonal flow looks to build in with height rises \noccurring across much of the Four Corners region. This will bring \nquiescent weather to the area with dry and mild conditions expected \nthrough at least Sunday. Temperatures Wednesday and Thursday \nafternoons will run near-normal before increasing to broadly 10F \nabove normal Friday into early next week. \n\nLong-range guidance does hint at some troughing looking to occur \nearly next week, potentially ushering in another instance of active \nweather. This is a particularly hairy forecast to iron out given the \nactive EPAC, as any storm that forms within the basin could impact \nmoisture return across the forecast area. At this time, it appears \nthat there is a low (~20% or less) chance that we see moisture make \nit up to southern UT at least with the next system, though this \nforecast will more than likely change through the next several days.",
